8.latex:
\textbf{Dimensionality Reduction}

If there are $d$ dimensions (attributes) in the data, dimensionality reduction refers to methods that transform the data to fewer dimensions, i.e., $d'<d$

Why reduce the dimensionality?
\begin{itemize}
    \item To discard irrelevant features
    \item To visualize high dimensional data
    \item To reflect "intrinsic" dimensionality of data, which may be smaller than the original number of attributes
\end{itemize}

10.latex:
\textbf{Singular value decomposition}

A singular value decomposition (SVD) of an $n \times d$ matrix $A$ expresses the matrix as the product of three "simple" matrices:

$A = USV^T$

\begin{itemize}
    \item $U$ is an $n \times n$ orthogonal matrix
    \item $V$ is a $d \times d$ orthogonal matrix
    \item $S$ is an $n \times d$ diagonal matrix with nonnegative entries sorted from high to low
\end{itemize}

11.latex:
\textbf{SVD}

Every matrix $A$ has a SVD
\begin{itemize}
    \item The columns of $U$ are left singular vectors of $A$
    \item The columns of $V$ (i.e. the rows of $V^T$) are right singular vectors of $A$
    \item The entries of $S$ are the singular values of $A$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
$
\begin{array}{ccc}
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
& = &
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
\\
A & & U \\
\end{array}
$
\end{center}

\begin{center}
$
\begin{array}{ccc}
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
& = &
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
\\
& & S \\
\end{array}
$
\end{center}

\begin{center}
$
\begin{array}{ccc}
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
& = &
\begin{array}{c c}
n & d \\
\end{array}
\\
& & V^T \\
\end{array}
$
\end{center}

14.latex:
\textbf{SVD vs PCA}

PCA and SVD are closely related

\begin{itemize}
    \item SVD is a general operation defined on all matrices
    \item Principle component analysis (PCA) refers to a data analysis technique for dimensionality reduction. To apply PCA to matrix $A$, the task reduces to computing the SVD of $A$
\end{itemize}

We will see that SVD gives strictly more information than PCA, namely the matrix $U$

15.latex:
\textbf{Principle component analysis}

PCA is a linear dimensionality reduction method dating back to Pearson (1901)

One of the most useful techniques in exploratory data analysis

17.latex:
\textbf{PCA: High level approach}

Given data matrix $X$ with $p$ dimensions (attributes)

\begin{itemize}
    \item Mean center each column of $X$ (i.e. subtract from each attribute value the mean of that attribute)
    \item Compute the $p \times p$ covariance matrix: $\Sigma = X^TX$
    \item Find eigenvectors and eigenvalues of the symmetric covariance matrix $\Sigma$
    The eigenvectors correspond to the principle components of $X$
    \item Select the $m$ eigenvectors with largest eigenvalues to reduce dimensionality
\end{itemize}

