43.latex:
\textbf{A Baseline Predictor}

We have expectations on the rating by user $i$ of movie $j$, even without estimating $i$'s attitude towards movies like $j$

Rating scale of user $u$

\begin{itemize}
    \item Values of other ratings user gave recently (day-specific mood, anchoring, multi-user accounts)
    \item Selection bias; related to number of ratings user gave on the same day ("frequency")
\end{itemize}

44.latex:
\textbf{Modeling Systematic Biases}

$r_{ij} \approx \mu + b_i + b_j + u_i \cdot v_j$ \\
Rating: Mean rating + bias for user $i$ + bias for movie $j$ + user-movie interactions \\
\textbf{Example:} \\
\begin{itemize}
    \item Mean rating $\mu = 3.7$
    \item You are a critical reviewer; your ratings are 1 lower than the mean: $b_i = -1$
    \item Star Wars gets a mean rating of 0.5 higher than average movie: $b_j = 0.5$
    \item Predicted rating for you on Star Wars: $3.7 - 1 + 0.5 = 3.2$
\end{itemize}

