26.latex:
\textbf{Group Fairness}

Equalize two groups $S$, $T$ at the level of outcomes

\begin{itemize}
    \item For example, $S$=minority, $T$=everyone else
    \item $P(\text{outcome } o | S) = P(\text{outcome } o | T)$
\end{itemize}

This is not strong enough as a notion of fairness

\begin{itemize}
    \item Sometimes desirable, but can be abused
    \item Self-fulfilling prophecy: select smartest students in $T$, random students in $S$, then students in $T$ will perform better
\end{itemize}

27.latex:
\textbf{Individual fairness}

Treat similar individuals similarly

\begin{itemize}
    \item Similar for the purpose of the classification task
    \item Similarly distributed over outcomes
\end{itemize}

How to do this is a current area of research.

