33.latex:
\textbf{Intelligible Models for HealthCare}

Caruana et al. 2015

Rule-based system used in the study learned the
rule \texttt{HasAsthma(x)} $\implies$ \texttt{LowerRisk(x)}

\begin{itemize}
    \item i.e., patients who have a history of asthma have
    lower risk of dying from pneumonia than the
    general population -- which is counterintuitive
\end{itemize}

This reflected a true pattern in the training data

\begin{itemize}
    \item Patients with history of asthma were admitted
    directly to the ICU with aggressive care that
    effectively lowered their risk of dying
\end{itemize}

But causal reasoning is incorrect... asthmatics have
much higher risk if not hospitalized

If rule-based systems have this problem,
then neural networks probably made similar
mistakes... but even more difficult to interpret

34.latex:
\textbf{Detecting and removing bias from models}

Bias is in the data, not the leaning algorithm

Models trained on data will lean any biases in the data

\begin{itemize}
    \item ML for resume processing will learn gender bias if data has a gender bias
    \item ML for recidivism prediction will learn race bias if data has a race bias
    \item ...
\end{itemize}

How to deal with bias using transparent models

\begin{itemize}
    \item Must keep bias features in data when model is trained
    \item Remove what was learned from these bias features after training
\end{itemize}

If offending bias variables are eliminated prior to training:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Often can't tell you still have a problem
    \item Makes it harder to correct the problem
\end{itemize}

EU General Data Protection Regulation (in effect 2018)

\begin{itemize}
    \item Article 9 makes it more difficult to use personal data revealing racial or ethnic origin and other "special categories"
\end{itemize}

