42.latex:
\textbf{Evaluation Approach: Subjective}

Select a small number of "canonical" members
of each cluster for inspection

\begin{itemize}
    \item E.g., in k-means select the examples that are
    closest to cluster centers
\end{itemize}

Hopefully, the chosen examples can be used to
interpret the characteristics of each cluster (with
domain knowledge)

43.latex:
\textbf{Evaluation Approach: Objective}

Evaluate the clusters using known class labels

If you have class labels why cluster?
\begin{itemize}
    \item The labeled dataset is usually hand-labeled specifically for evaluation
    \item Cluster the larger dataset automatically
    \item Assess the clustering of the hand labeled dataset in the automatic clustering
\end{itemize}

44.latex:
\textbf{Evaluation via Class Labels: Purity}

Purity: a measure of the extent to which cluster/groups (G) contain objects of a particular class (C)

\begin{itemize}
    \item Each cluster is assigned the class according to the most numerous class of its elements
    \item The purity of each cluster $i$ in $G$ is determined by the number of examples of class $j$ ($N_j$) inside each cluster:
    \[
    \text{purity}(C, G) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{K} \max_{j} N_j, \quad N_j \in G_i
    \]
\end{itemize}

High purity is easy to achieve when the number of clusters is large

